From a0c12281109f8f7cfcfed3eaedf420e84ac35c22 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lynxtron Scripts <scripts@lynxtron>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 17:10:46 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Adapt lynx for HarmonyOS

Build and runtime adaptations needed to bring Lynx up inside a HarmonyOS
HAP, on top of the desktop "V8 + NS" model the other lynxtron patches
establish.

Build:
  - core/BUILD.gn: the OHOS NDK clang rejects the VLA extension lynx uses,
    so silence -Wvla-cxx-extension for the harmony config.
  - clay: reuse the linux external-bitmap shared image, take the GLES3
    headers, and treat headless harmony like the other headless desktop
    targets for vsync and the native view service fallback.
  - platform/embedder/resource: harmony loads JS sources the desktop way.
  - jsvm_dyn_load.h: drop the deviceinfo.h OS-version gate; that header is
    not in the NDK sysroot being built against.
  - text/*_harmony.h: in C++20 a u8"" literal is char8_t[], but the harmony
    text APIs take const char*. Spell the same UTF-8 bytes explicitly.

Runtime:
  - base/src/BUILD.gn: message_loop_harmony.cc must compile against Node's
    bundled uv.h (1.51) -- the libuv actually linked into liblynxtron.so --
    and not the OHOS system libuv (1.44) in the NDK sysroot. Their
    uv_loop_t layouts differ, so the system header makes
    uv_async_send(&looper_->wq_async) write at the wrong offset and crash
    in uv__io_poll.
  - js_thread_config_getter_harmony.cc: do not adopt the ArkTS runtime's uv
    loop. It belongs to the system libuv, while Node's uv_run drives the
    loop, and the same layout mismatch SIGSEGVs on the Lynx_JS thread.
    Passing nullptr makes MessageLoopHarmony create its own Node-libuv loop.
  - runtime_manager.cc: run Lynx's background JS on Node's V8 rather than
    the OHOS JSVM/ArkTS engine, and register the V8 napi proxy factory.
    Its Attach() calls the real napi_attach_v8; the JSVM path attached
    through a no-op stub, leaving the napi method table null.
  - v8_isolate_wrapper_node_impl.cc: read the thread's uv loop through
    MessageLoopHarmony on harmony. message_loop_node.cc is only built for
    the desktop platforms, and the two loops are not layout compatible --
    MessageLoopNode embeds a uv_loop_t and returns its address, while
    MessageLoopHarmony holds a uv_loop_t* and returns the pointer -- so
    reading one as the other hands node::NewIsolate a wild loop and
    crashes in uv_async_init.
  - platform_harmony.cc: scan /system/fonts for the system typefaces.
    SkFontMgr::RefDefault() resolves to the custom-directory factory, whose
    built-in prefix is the Linux /usr/share/fonts/; on a device that path
    does not exist, so the font manager has no families and every <text>
    node lays out to nothing -- the UI renders images and boxes but no
    glyphs.
